WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.190 --> 00:00:06.526
On November 22, 1995, Toy Story 
was released in theaters in the US.

00:00:06.526 --> 00:00:11.730
You couldn’t take it home to watch it
on your VCR until October 29th, 1996 --

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.119
11 months after it opened.

00:00:14.119 --> 00:00:17.080
This is called the theatrical window:

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.820
when a movie is shown only in theaters.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.710
And it’s gotten way smaller over time.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.177
Toy Story 4 was released in June of 2019.

00:00:26.177 --> 00:00:30.420
To watch it at home, you only had to wait
about 3 months.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.999
This shrinking window is the result 
of a decades-long fight

00:00:33.999 --> 00:00:37.059
between movie theaters and movie studios.

00:00:37.059 --> 00:00:40.000
But how much smaller
can the window get?

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.599
Are movie theaters close to becoming 
a thing of the past?

00:00:45.726 --> 00:00:48.384
Thanks to a Supreme Court
case from the 1940s,

00:00:48.384 --> 00:00:53.057
movie studios and theater owners 
in the US are separate entities.

00:00:53.057 --> 00:00:57.045
For a while, the only way to see a movie 
was to go to a theater.

00:00:57.045 --> 00:00:58.367
But pretty soon...

00:00:58.367 --> 00:00:59.290
"Televison--"

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:00.000
"--VHS--"

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:00.878
"--DVD--"

00:01:02.193 --> 00:01:07.272
The days of theaters being the only way 
to watch a movie are long gone.

00:01:07.272 --> 00:01:09.426
To defend against those new competitors,

00:01:09.426 --> 00:01:11.792
theater owners have struck deals
with movie studios

00:01:11.792 --> 00:01:14.000
 to give them a window of exclusivity

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.930
before a movie could be seen 
in any other format.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.830
Movie studios agreed because 
it worked for them, too.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.085
ZORADI: We created these windows because

00:01:22.085 --> 00:01:25.730
they were really good for 
the motion picture business.

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:28.497
That's Mark Zoradi, a former Disney executive.

00:01:28.497 --> 00:01:31.419
ZORADI: ...from 1980 until 2010...

00:01:31.419 --> 00:01:32.440
But today, he's...

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.770
ZORADI: ...the CEO of Cinemark Theaters.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.237
The third largest theater chain 
in the US and Canada. 

00:01:38.237 --> 00:01:41.283
Theater owners generally want 
a longer exclusive window,

00:01:41.283 --> 00:01:45.000
because they only make money 
while a film is in theaters.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.892
And even then they’re splitting
ticket profits, roughly 50/50.

00:01:49.892 --> 00:01:51.911
But movie studios have the opportunity

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:55.733
to keep making money long after a film
is shown in theaters.

00:01:55.733 --> 00:02:00.920
In fact, it’s become essential for studios to
recoup their investments on expensive films.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.000
ZORADI: In order for you to break even,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.211
then you've got to go right down the line,
with multiple bites 

00:02:05.211 --> 00:02:07.928
in order to make that all back.

00:02:07.928 --> 00:02:11.654
In the beginning, the minimum
theatrical window was quite long.

00:02:11.654 --> 00:02:13.128
But obviously...

00:02:13.128 --> 00:02:14.730
CORCORAN: Over time, that's shrunk.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.070
With the introduction of DVDs,

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.100
you had a window at roughly
six months.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.909
ZORADI: The window, prior to
the pandemic, was essentially

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:26.892
74 days before a movie would go to 
electronic sell through.

00:02:26.892 --> 00:02:30.753
That means a digital release you can buy,
on a platform like iTunes.

00:02:30.753 --> 00:02:34.812
ZORADI: And then at 90 days they
would take it to DVD, VOD, 

00:02:34.812 --> 00:02:38.937
and then after that, they would take it to
pay television or streaming.

00:02:38.937 --> 00:02:41.411
That's kind of how the sequence is.

00:02:41.411 --> 00:02:44.420
But studios haven’t always 
gotten their way.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:49.148
In 2010, Warner Bros.'s parent company 
proposed a window of 30 days.

00:02:49.148 --> 00:02:51.843
Which theater owners didn’t agree to.

00:02:51.843 --> 00:02:54.200
Netflix challenged the window 
a few years later,

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.185
planning a movie release in IMAX
and on Netflix at the same time.

00:02:58.185 --> 00:03:01.249
Major theater chains banned their films.

00:03:01.249 --> 00:03:04.211
One studio has been really persistent though.

00:03:04.211 --> 00:03:07.602
RUBIN: Universal has been probably 
the most public and aggressive 

00:03:07.602 --> 00:03:10.078
about wanting to shorten 
the theatrical window.

00:03:10.078 --> 00:03:14.623
In 2011, Universal made plans to
release the film Tower Heist on demand 

00:03:14.623 --> 00:03:16.786
30 days after it hit theaters.

00:03:16.786 --> 00:03:20.186
Some chains threatened to 
not show the film at all.

00:03:20.186 --> 00:03:25.146
Universal ultimately backed down,
and kept their window at 90 days.

00:03:25.798 --> 00:03:31.381
Ultimately, theaters need studios, 
because they need movies to show.

00:03:31.381 --> 00:03:35.359
Studios need theaters to make big money
and get big press.

00:03:35.359 --> 00:03:38.472
And that mutual need kept things stable.

00:03:38.472 --> 00:03:39.734
For a while.

00:03:39.734 --> 00:03:40.735
Until...

00:03:40.735 --> 00:03:42.370
"Theaters shut down due to the global pandemic."

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:44.470
"Movie theaters were forced 
to close their doors."

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:47.070
"They've been trying to survive the coronavirus."

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:48.670
(Overlapping) "Bankruptcy."

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:50.740
"...in AMC's future."

00:03:51.444 --> 00:03:54.367
RUBIN: The power dynamic shifted a lot 
during the pandemic.

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:57.560
In 2020, studios had newfound leverage

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.600
and one company struck 
while the iron was hot.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.167
RUBIN: Universal had this movie,
Trolls: World Tour.

00:04:03.167 --> 00:04:04.511
And they had two options.

00:04:04.511 --> 00:04:08.910
One, they could delay it, or two they could
test what they've always wanted to do

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:12.912
and put the movie in theaters and on video
on demand on the same day.

00:04:12.912 --> 00:04:17.391
And the caveat there is that there weren't
any movie theaters open to play it.

00:04:17.391 --> 00:04:22.380
Trolls: World Tour quickly became 
the highest grossing digital release ever.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:25.840
It made more money for Universal 
in 3 weeks at home

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.647
than the original film did 
in its theatrical run.

00:04:29.647 --> 00:04:33.131
NBCUniversal’s head of film 
told the Wall Street Journal:

00:04:33.131 --> 00:04:38.320
“As soon as theaters reopen, we expect 
to release movies on both formats.”

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:39.342
In other words:

00:04:39.342 --> 00:04:43.346
in theaters and on demand at the same time.

00:04:43.346 --> 00:04:45.613
The two biggest theater chains in the US

00:04:45.613 --> 00:04:49.860
fired back by saying they’d never show 
Universal films again.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.202
But things were different now.

00:04:52.202 --> 00:04:57.437
The major film studios had restructured
to make streaming their primary focus.

00:04:57.437 --> 00:05:04.630
Warner Bros. released their entire 2021 slate
in theaters and on HBO Max at the same time.

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:09.390
Disney and Universal made a similar move.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:14.664
CORCORAN: With that upset came time
for theater owners and Universal

00:05:14.664 --> 00:05:18.019
and the other studios, frankly, to
start having those conversations

00:05:18.019 --> 00:05:23.088
and working out about what the future looks
like, and what a post-pandemic model is.

00:05:23.088 --> 00:05:27.440
In 2021 the major theater chains
made deals to shorten the window.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:33.566
Disney, Paramount, and Warner Bros. agreed
to a 45 day window in 2022 and onwards.

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:38.078
But Universal got its own deal 
with the big theater chains.

00:05:38.078 --> 00:05:42.040
They agreed to give theaters a portion of
their video on demand revenue.

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:46.800
And in return they secured 
one of the shortest windows ever.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.626
31 days for their biggest movies,
17 days on their smaller movies.

00:05:52.361 --> 00:05:54.070
But one thing that’s noticeably absent

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:58.389
from all of these deals is any sign of 
a same-day release strategy,

00:05:58.389 --> 00:06:03.202
where a movie comes out on demand 
and in theaters at the same time.

00:06:03.202 --> 00:06:05.870
At what seemed to be
their most powerful moment,

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:11.399
studios decided to keep the 
theatrical window in place.

00:06:11.399 --> 00:06:16.285
That's partly because movie theaters are still
pretty big money makers for studios.

00:06:16.285 --> 00:06:21.713
But it's also because movie theaters can sort
of change how the public sees a movie.

00:06:21.713 --> 00:06:25.580
CORCORAN: Streaming customers value 
theatrical releases more highly.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:27.732
It carries with it sort of an aura of quality

00:06:27.732 --> 00:06:29.580
ZORADI: It "eventizes" a movie.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:36.370
It's a shared experience that helps become
almost like a zeitgeist within the culture.

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:41.596
The pandemic shifted the power dynamic towards
movie studios and away from theaters.

00:06:41.596 --> 00:06:46.680
But it also showed that 
they still need each other.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:50.000
That doesn’t necessarily mean 
theaters will be around forever.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:56.581
But if they can survive a pandemic, maybe
they're stronger than we thought.

